Form 1 — converging ends (sorted pair-sum), verified:

```js
// O(n): each comparison eliminates an entire candidate row
function pairWithSum(sorted, target) {
  let lo = 0, hi = sorted.length - 1;
  while (lo < hi) {                          // meet-strictly-in-the-middle
    const sum = sorted[lo] + sorted[hi];
    if (sum === target) return [lo, hi];
    if (sum < target) lo++;                   // need bigger → move left up
    else hi--;                               // need smaller → move right down
  }
}
// Why elimination works: sum too small → EVERY pair using sorted[lo]
// with a smaller hi is also too small — lo is dead, advance it.
```

Form 2 — fast/slow writer (in-place removal):
```js
// remove duplicates in place — slow marks the kept boundary
function dedupeSorted(nums) {
  let w = 0;
  for (let r = 0; r < nums.length; r++) {
    if (r === 0 || nums[r] !== nums[r - 1]) nums[w++] = nums[r];
  }
  nums.length = w;                           // trim the tail
}
// O(n) time, O(1) space — the alternative filter() allocates a new array
```

Form 3 — tortoise/hare (cycle detection): slow moves 1, fast moves 2; in a cycle they MUST meet (the gap closes by 1 each step); to find the cycle START, reset one pointer to the head and move both at 1 — they meet at the entry (Floyd's algorithm, the same math that finds the duplicate number in the n+1-ints-in-range-n problem).

The recognizer (what to say in the interview): 'the answer needs a PAIR (or a window) and each comparison can PERMANENTLY discard one candidate' — sorted pair-sum, in-place filtering, palindrome checks (ends converge comparing chars), container-with-most-water (greedy pointer moves). If discarding isn't provable, you need the hashmap instead.
